1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,640
How do you research NFL player props?

2
00:00:03,040 --> 00:00:04,880
Start with the market, not the model.

3
00:00:05,280 --> 00:00:09,220
Confirm that a current slate exists, then
verify the player,

4
00:00:09,300 --> 00:00:12,220
stat, exact line, platform, and timestamp.

5
00:00:13,120 --> 00:00:17,420
After that, research role, injuries,
matchup, and game environment.

6
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:21,310
Only then should a model direction or
confidence score enter the

7
00:00:21,320 --> 00:00:25,890
decision. Here is a repeatable game-week
workflow you can use on

8
00:00:25,900 --> 00:00:28,260
Propeller, on the web or mobile.

9
00:00:29,040 --> 00:00:32,510
Because we are recording during the NFL
offseason,

10
00:00:32,520 --> 00:00:36,100
the current board correctly shows that no
live slate is available.

11
00:00:36,800 --> 00:00:40,410
To make the features concrete, this
walkthrough then switches to a

12
00:00:40,420 --> 00:00:45,150
clearly labeled Josh Allen passing-yards
example built from Propeller's archived

13
00:00:45,160 --> 00:00:49,660
2025 NFL data. It demonstrates the
interface,

14
00:00:50,120 --> 00:00:53,400
but it was not a live or published pregame
recommendation.

15
00:00:54,160 --> 00:00:55,700
Step one is availability.

16
00:00:56,620 --> 00:00:59,420
Open the NFL board and check its current
state.

17
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:02,780
During the offseason, while a slate is
being built,

18
00:01:03,020 --> 00:01:04,920
or on a day with no listed games,

19
00:01:05,460 --> 00:01:07,240
there may be nothing current to analyze.

20
00:01:08,020 --> 00:01:09,260
That is useful information.

21
00:01:10,120 --> 00:01:13,220
Do not replace a missing current line with
an old screenshot,

22
00:01:13,740 --> 00:01:17,000
an archived result, or a number from
another platform.

23
00:01:17,660 --> 00:01:21,670
When a verified slate is available, open a
prop and identify

24
00:01:21,680 --> 00:01:25,520
five things before reading any analysis:
the player,

25
00:01:25,840 --> 00:01:29,160
the stat category, the exact line, the
platform,

26
00:01:29,540 --> 00:01:31,480
and the time the source was updated.

27
00:01:32,120 --> 00:01:35,660
Lines can differ across platforms and can
move during the week.

28
00:01:36,240 --> 00:01:39,690
Research built around the wrong number is
research for a different

29
00:01:39,700 --> 00:01:43,260
decision. Step two is role and
opportunity.

30
00:01:43,980 --> 00:01:46,110
Recent box scores tell you what happened,

31
00:01:46,120 --> 00:01:47,980
but they do not always tell you what
changed.

32
00:01:48,760 --> 00:01:50,760
For a receiver, look for routes, targets,

33
00:01:51,300 --> 00:01:52,460
alignment, and snap participation.

34
00:01:53,380 --> 00:01:55,170
For a running back, look at carry volume,

35
00:01:55,180 --> 00:01:57,660
passing-game work, and goal-line usage.

36
00:01:58,260 --> 00:02:01,010
For a quarterback, consider attempts,
designed runs,

37
00:02:01,020 --> 00:02:02,800
and the expected game environment.

38
00:02:03,620 --> 00:02:06,970
The key question is not simply, “Did this
player clear the

39
00:02:06,980 --> 00:02:11,350
line last week?” It is, “Is the role that
created those

40
00:02:11,360 --> 00:02:16,360
opportunities still present this week?”
Step three is injury context.

41
00:02:17,020 --> 00:02:19,640
Start with the official NFL injury report,

42
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:23,600
then confirm game status and the inactive
list as kickoff approaches.

43
00:02:24,360 --> 00:02:26,880
Also look beyond the player named on the
prop.

44
00:02:27,480 --> 00:02:30,660
A teammate being limited or ruled out can
change routes,

45
00:02:31,060 --> 00:02:34,750
target share, carry volume, protection,
and red-zone work.

46
00:02:34,760 --> 00:02:37,760
That is why injury research is a cascade,

47
00:02:38,500 --> 00:02:40,460
not a single yes-or-no flag.

48
00:02:41,500 --> 00:02:43,710
Step four is matchup and game environment.

49
00:02:43,720 --> 00:02:48,590
Position-specific defensive context can be
more useful than a broad team

50
00:02:48,600 --> 00:02:53,100
ranking. Then add likely pace,
pass-versus-run environment,

51
00:02:53,560 --> 00:02:56,520
weather for outdoor games, and possible
game scripts.

52
00:02:57,180 --> 00:02:59,750
None of those inputs proves an outcome by
itself.

53
00:02:59,760 --> 00:03:03,490
The goal is to understand the range of
ways the game

54
00:03:03,500 --> 00:03:06,840
could develop and which assumptions the
prop depends on.

55
00:03:07,580 --> 00:03:09,840
Now inspect the Propeller agent breakdown.

56
00:03:10,580 --> 00:03:13,060
Each agent is a specialized research lens.

57
00:03:13,680 --> 00:03:17,920
In this historical example, the saved
breakdown includes DVOA,

58
00:03:18,120 --> 00:03:23,060
game script, hit rate, injury, matchup,
sportsbook-margin adjustment,

59
00:03:23,460 --> 00:03:27,350
variance, and volume. The agents shown can
vary with the evidence

60
00:03:27,360 --> 00:03:31,510
and model version. Signals without usable
evidence are skipped,

61
00:03:31,520 --> 00:03:35,340
and the available signals are combined
into the displayed direction.

62
00:03:36,480 --> 00:03:38,020
Do not look only for agreement.

63
00:03:38,480 --> 00:03:41,160
Disagreement can be the most useful part
of the breakdown.

64
00:03:41,940 --> 00:03:46,070
A favorable matchup beside uncertain
volume is a reason to investigate

65
00:03:46,080 --> 00:03:51,170
the role. A market signal that conflicts
with injury context is

66
00:03:51,180 --> 00:03:53,690
a reason to check when both sources were
updated.

67
00:03:53,700 --> 00:03:57,300
And if an injury signal has no usable
result,

68
00:03:57,800 --> 00:03:59,920
that does not prove the player is healthy.

69
00:04:00,500 --> 00:04:03,460
It means the official status still needs
to be verified.

70
00:04:04,700 --> 00:04:07,740
Next, read the direction before the
confidence number.

71
00:04:08,340 --> 00:04:12,250
The label MORE, or OVER, means the
available signals lean above

72
00:04:12,260 --> 00:04:15,210
the listed line. The label LESS, or UNDER,

73
00:04:15,220 --> 00:04:17,070
means they lean below it.

74
00:04:17,080 --> 00:04:21,830
Propeller’s confidence score runs from
fifty to one hundred and shows

75
00:04:21,840 --> 00:04:26,160
how strongly the available model signals
support that displayed direction.

76
00:04:27,300 --> 00:04:30,080
That score is not a calibrated win
probability.

77
00:04:30,920 --> 00:04:34,350
A confidence score of seventy-five does
not mean the prop has

78
00:04:34,360 --> 00:04:36,760
a seventy-five-percent chance of winning.

79
00:04:37,360 --> 00:04:40,000
It is also different from market-implied
probability,

80
00:04:40,480 --> 00:04:42,700
which is estimated from listed prices.

81
00:04:43,240 --> 00:04:48,080
Keep direction, confidence, line, and
market probability separate.

82
00:04:49,020 --> 00:04:51,320
Line movement deserves the same care.

83
00:04:51,900 --> 00:04:54,160
A changing number tells you the market
changed;

84
00:04:54,800 --> 00:04:56,100
it does not tell you why.

85
00:04:56,680 --> 00:05:00,060
The move could reflect injury news, role
information,

86
00:05:00,640 --> 00:05:02,730
limits, or ordinary market adjustment.

87
00:05:02,740 --> 00:05:07,610
Recheck the current line and timestamp
instead of assuming movement confirms

88
00:05:07,620 --> 00:05:12,340
the model. Before making a decision, run
one final checklist.

89
00:05:13,020 --> 00:05:13,960
Is the slate current?

90
00:05:14,580 --> 00:05:17,340
Does the player, stat, line, and platform
match?

91
00:05:17,740 --> 00:05:19,500
Is the expected role intact?

92
00:05:20,140 --> 00:05:23,060
Are the injury report and inactive status
current?

93
00:05:23,640 --> 00:05:27,120
Do the matchup and game environment
support the same assumptions?

94
00:05:27,760 --> 00:05:29,400
What do the agents disagree about?

95
00:05:29,840 --> 00:05:32,020
And what new information would make you
pass?

96
00:05:33,280 --> 00:05:34,430
That last question matters.

97
00:05:34,440 --> 00:05:38,220
A good research process needs a clear
no-action outcome.

98
00:05:39,040 --> 00:05:42,480
If the line moved too far, the player’s
role is uncertain,

99
00:05:43,060 --> 00:05:46,760
or the evidence is stale, walking away is
a valid conclusion.

100
00:05:48,200 --> 00:05:51,190
Then repeat the freshness check close to
kickoff.

101
00:05:51,200 --> 00:05:54,420
NFL context can change after your first
review,

102
00:05:54,880 --> 00:05:58,150
so the final decision should use the
current line and the

103
00:05:58,160 --> 00:06:00,340
newest official information available.

104
